      "annotatedScript": "[SLOW DOWN] In the autumn of 1998, the city of Ghent smelled of wet cobblestones, roasted chestnuts, and coal smoke drifting off the Lys River. [BEAT] Every morning at seven, Willem Verhoeven climbed two hundred and fourteen spiral steps into the municipal belfry with a leather tool roll tucked under his arm and a thermos of black coffee in his coat pocket. Willem was fifty-four, a third-generation horologist whose knuckles were permanently stained with graphite grease. His job was simple to describe and nearly impossible to execute: he kept the city's public time. [PAUSE 1.5s] For thirty years, Willem had oiled the iron escapements, filed the brass pawls, and listened to the wind rattle the louvers. Yet one problem had defeated every technician before him. Across the historic center stood [EMPHASIS: four rival parishes]\u2014St. Bavo, St. Nicholas, St. Michael, and St. James. Each tower housed a massive bronze bell cast in a different century, and each parish council guarded its independence like a medieval charter. [LEAN IN] For decades, noon in Ghent had been a small civil war. St. Bavo's bell struck forty seconds early because its warden trusted a pocket watch from 1922. St. Nicholas struck two minutes late. St. Michael's chime dragged behind by four. [BEAT] When the city's cultural director, a brisk woman named Claire De Smet, summoned Willem to Town Hall on a Tuesday morning, she slid a single sheet of paper across the oak table. The European Heritage Committee was arriving in ten days. She wanted all four parish bells synchronized to strike noon within [EMPHASIS: a sixty-second window]. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"Make them agree, Willem,\" she said, tapping the paper. \"One city, one noon.\" [SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Willem's first attempt lasted forty-eight hours and ended in polite fury. He began at St. Nicholas, climbing into the drafty oak frame with a strobe tachometer and a brass plumb line. The parish warden, Father Hendrickx, watched from the trapdoor with his arms crossed. When Willem suggested adjusting the fly-fan governor to shave off two minutes of lag, the priest shook his head. \"Our bell has been three minutes behind Bavo since the war,\" Hendrickx said. \"If we strike with them, people will think we surrendered.\" [BEAT] At St. Michael's, the verger refused to let Willem touch the counterweight rope at all. At St. James, the bell-master agreed to cooperate, then quietly reset his pendulum the moment Willem's boots hit the bottom stair. [LOWER VOICE] By Thursday afternoon, Willem decided to force the issue. He spent six hours calibrating all four mechanical striking trains to the exact same radio-controlled atomic signal. At 11:59 on Friday, he stood in the middle of the Korenmarkt, coat collar turned up against the drizzle, waiting for his masterpiece. [PAUSE 2s] At noon, all four towers fired within three seconds of one another. [STILLNESS] It was a disaster. [BEAT] The four bronze bells\u2014tuned to different keys, swinging at different tempos\u2014collided in the narrow stone canyon above the canal. Instead of grandeur, the sound bounced off the guildhalls and turned into [EMPHASIS: muddy, bronze-colored static]. Pigeons panicked. A caf\u00e9 waiter dropped a tray of glasses. Claire De Smet called Willem's workshop before the last echo had even died. \"It sounded like a train wreck in a cathedral,\" she said. \"Fix it by Monday, or I hire a firm from Brussels.\" [SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] That night, Willem sat alone in his workshop above the fish market. A single green-shaded lamp lit his bench, where dozens of spare escapement wheels lay like silver coins on oiled felt. Rain ticked against the leaded glass. [BEAT] His hands were aching. For the first time in thirty years, he wondered if he was simply too old for modern expectations. The physics were against him. Four bells of different weights could not be forced into the same millisecond without destroying their resonance. Even if the priests agreed\u2014and they never would\u2014the stone architecture of Ghent itself rejected unison. [LOWER VOICE] At 1:30 in the morning, Willem pulled on his wool coat, picked up his notebook, and walked out into the empty square. He stood on St. Michael's Bridge, watching the black water slide under the arches. He was ready to write his resignation letter at dawn. [PAUSE 1.5s] Then, from the far end of the canal, a barge horn sounded\u2014a low, flat blast that rolled across the water and faded cleanly into the fog. Three seconds later, a tugboat answered from the lock, a higher note, crisp and distinct. Neither vessel spoke over the other. Both were heard clearly across a mile of wet stone. [STILLNESS] [SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] Willem froze on the bridge, his breath pluming in the cold air. He opened his damp notebook and drew four vertical lines, each spaced fifteen seconds apart. [PAUSE 2s] At eight o'clock Saturday morning, he did not climb a single tower. Instead, he invited all four parish wardens to a bakery across from Town Hall, ordered four pots of coffee, and laid his notebook between the sugar bowls. [BEAT] Father Hendrickx leaned back immediately. \"If you are asking us to strike at the exact same second again, Willem, the answer is no.\" [WARM SMILE] \"I am not,\" Willem said quietly. \"Yesterday I tried to make you sound like one voice. It was ugly. Today I want to give each of you fifteen seconds of the sky.\" [BEAT] He tapped the first line. \"St. Bavo has the deepest bronze. You open noon at 12:00:00\u2014four clean strikes, twelve seconds, then silence.\" He tapped the second line. \"At 12:00:15, St. Nicholas answers. Your tone is brighter; it will carry right over the canal.\" He moved to the third and fourth lines. \"St. Michael at 12:00:30. St. James closes the minute at 12:00:45.\" [LOWER VOICE] The table went quiet. The verger from St. Michael's squinted at the page. \"And nobody strikes while another is ringing?\" \"Nobody,\" Willem said. \"[EMPHASIS: Not unison, but sequence]. Every parish keeps its own voice, its own key, and its own pride\u2014all inside one sixty-second frame.\" [SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] It took Willem thirty-six hours of climbing, filing, and counter-weighting to lock the four governors into their fifteen-second intervals. On Monday at 11:58, he stood once more on St. Michael's Bridge, Claire De Smet beside him, her hands jammed deep into her trench coat pockets. [SLOW DOWN] At 12:00:00, St. Bavo's great bell rolled out across the rooftops\u2014deep, unhurried, unmistakable. Three seconds of clean air followed. Then St. Nicholas answered from the east, bright and sharp. Fifteen seconds later, St. Michael's took the baton, and at 12:00:45, St. James closed the minute with a final, ringing chord that hung over the river like a signature. [PAUSE 2s] [STILLNESS] Across the square, shoppers stopped mid-stride. Nobody flinched. Nobody covered their ears. For the first time in living memory, [EMPHASIS: every voice heard in full]\u2014distinct, unhurried, and unmistakably part of the same city. Claire De Smet let out a long breath and looked at Willem. \"They didn't agree on a single note,\" she murmured. \"No,\" Willem said, clicking his stopwatch shut. \"They just agreed on when to listen.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] For the next twenty years, until Willem finally hung up his leather tool roll, noon in Ghent belonged to four rival parishes who never once learned to sing in unison\u2014and never needed to.",
