      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] In May of 2019, Callum Reid was forty-two years old, flying a battered de Havilland Beaver on floats out of Thompson, Manitoba. [BEAT] Every morning at five-fifteen, before the coffee in the hangar thermos had cooled, Callum walked the dock with a grease pencil tucked behind his ear and a laminated clipboard under his arm. The Beaver smelled of avgas, wet cedar, and old canvas. Outside, the Burntwood River was usually a clean slate of dark water bordered by black spruce. [PAUSE 1.5s] That week, it was neither. Three days of warm rain on top of late snowpack had pushed the river over its banks, swallowing forty miles of logging roads and turning the low-lying towns of Birch Narrows and Pelican Rapids into islands of mud and plywood. [EMPHASIS: forty miles of brown water] [LEAN IN] Inside the dispatch shack, the whiteboard was covered in red marker. Insulin for Birch Narrows. Pediatric antibiotics for Pelican Rapids. Two cases of sterile dressing and a portable oxygen concentrator that weighed thirty-eight pounds on a good day. Callum's job was simple on paper: fly the supplies in, drop them at the gravel strip above the high-water mark, and get back before the afternoon squalls rolled off the lake. [BEAT] Simple, until the gravel strip at Birch Narrows disappeared under six inches of silt and driftwood.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] By ten-thirty that morning, Callum had already made one run to Pelican Rapids and was banking south toward Birch Narrows when the radio crackled. It was Maren Lindqvist, the nurse-in-charge at the Birch Narrows clinic, her voice thin and clipped over the static. \"Callum, the lower road is gone. The clinic generator is sitting on pallets, and the water is at the second step.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Callum glanced at his fuel gauge, then at the gray ceiling of cloud pressing down to eight hundred feet. \"Maren, I have the insulin and the oxygen,\" he said into the boom mic. \"Tell me the gravel strip by the school is dry.\" [BEAT] There was a long hiss on the line. \"It's not dry,\" Maren said. \"It's half-covered in spruce limbs, and the county grader is stuck at the north end.\" [LOWER VOICE] Callum banked the Beaver in a tight circle over the flooded trees. Below him, the strip looked less like a runway and more like a wet ribbon of gravel threading between two ditches. He lined up anyway, dropped the flaps, and brought the floats down toward the water-logged edge. [PAUSE 2s] At fifty feet, a crosswind gust caught the left wingtip and shoved the nose toward a stand of drowned alders. Callum shoved the throttle forward, the radial engine roaring back to life, and climbed out over the treetops with his knuckles white on the yoke. \"No good,\" he muttered to the empty cabin. \"Too narrow, too much debris.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By nine o'clock that night, Callum was sitting on an upturned milk crate in the Thompson hangar, a half-eaten sandwich on his knee and a map spread across two sawhorses. [BEAT] His eyes burned from twelve hours of staring into gray mist. Across the hangar, his dispatcher, a retired bush mechanic named Gordie Vance, was wiping grease from his hands with a shop rag. \"You can't put a Beaver down on a strip that's half-underwater,\" Gordie said, not looking up. \"You'll clip a float on a submerged stump, and then we're sending a chopper to pull you out instead of medicine.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Callum rubbed his temples. He knew Gordie was right. Worse, the weather forecast for dawn was calling for a low-pressure trough that would pin every floatplane in northern Manitoba to the dock for forty-eight hours. If the insulin and oxygen didn't reach Birch Narrows in the morning, the clinic would have to evacuate three patients by boat through debris-choked rapids. [LOWER VOICE] Callum stared at the cargo manifest on his clipboard. Thirty-eight pounds for the concentrator. Twelve pounds for the cooler. Four extra cases of bottled water he had thrown in out of habit. [EMPHASIS: weight is not the same as value] He had been trying to solve a weight-and-runway problem by flying harder, when the real problem was that he was carrying too much of the wrong thing for a landing zone that no longer existed.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] At five-forty the next morning, the rain had stopped, leaving a flat, pewter sky and a ceiling just high enough to see the river bends. Callum didn't load the Beaver the way he had the day before. He left the four cases of bottled water on the dock. He strapped the insulin cooler into the co-pilot seat and lashed the oxygen concentrator directly behind it, stripping sixty pounds of non-essential gear from the tail. [BEAT] Then he keyed the mic on the regional frequency. He didn't have time for a ten-minute briefing; the wind was already picking up from the northeast, giving him one clean pass before the squall line hit. [PAUSE 2s] \"Birch Narrows clinic, this is Lima-Charlie-Golf,\" Callum said, his voice level and unhurried. \"I have sixty seconds on the frequency before I drop below the ridge. Listen carefully. [EMPHASIS: sixty seconds on the frequency] Forget the gravel strip. I am not landing on the road. I am putting the floats down on the flooded ball diamond behind the school\u2014west to east, parallel to the fence. I need two people at the backstop with a bright tarp, and I need every boat cleared from the outfield. Do not talk back unless the fence is down. Say again: ball diamond, west to east, bright tarp at the backstop.\" [STILLNESS] He released the transmit button and waited. Five seconds. Ten seconds. Then Maren's voice came back, crisp and unmistakable: \"Copy, Callum. Tarp is going up now. Outfield is clear.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] [SLOW DOWN] When Callum cleared the spruce ridge two minutes later, he saw it immediately\u2014a rectangle of bright orange canvas stretched between the chain-link posts of the ball diamond. The outfield was four feet deep in brown water, flat as a dinner plate and free of stumps. [EMPHASIS: the strip was half a wingbeat wide] He dropped the flaps, eased the throttle back, and let the floats kiss the flooded grass with barely a splash. Before the prop had even stopped spinning, two volunteers in chest waders were sloshing out to the wing strut. [BEAT] Callum handed down the insulin cooler, then the oxygen concentrator, and didn't shut the engine off. He gave Maren a single nod through the side window, swung the nose back toward the centerfield fence, and lifted off into the mist with thirty seconds to spare before the squall line closed the valley. [PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] Later that week, when the water finally receded and the county grader pulled the mud off the main road, the orange tarp was still tied to the backstop at Birch Narrows. Callum never bragged about the flight, and he never put it in a company newsletter. Whenever a younger pilot asked him how he found a runway in forty miles of floodwater, Callum would just tap his laminated clipboard and say, \"You don't need a better speech. You just need to know what to leave on the dock.\"",
