      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] In the autumn of 1998, on a narrow street off Piazza San Ferdinando in Naples, Matteo Ferraro kept two sets of shears on his cutting table. [PAUSE 1.5s] The heavy brass pair was for the English worsted wool that arrived in bolts from Huddersfield. The smaller steel pair was for silk lining, pocket flaps, and the occasional loose thread of a client's temper. Matteo was sixty-one years old, slight of build, with spectacles that sat halfway down his nose and fingers permanently stained faint blue from tailor's chalk. [BEAT] Every morning at seven-thirty, he unlocked the tall green shutters of his third-floor workshop, brewed a single cup of espresso on a dented moka pot, and listened to the city wake up below\u2014vespas whining up the cobbles, fishmongers shouting prices, and the distant bell of San Ferdinando striking the half-hour. [PAUSE 1.5s] His reputation in Naples was simple: Matteo could cut a double-breasted jacket so clean that a man looked two inches taller and ten years calmer. [LEAN IN] Because of that, rivals came to him. Not together\u2014never together\u2014until the Tuesday morning in October when a municipal harbour contract split the city's two oldest shipping families in half. [EMPHASIS: sixty seconds of wool and silence] That was all Matteo ever asked for when he pinned a lapel, yet that Tuesday, even sixty seconds felt impossible.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] The first to arrive was Donato Esposito, sixty-four, broad-shouldered, wearing a charcoal overcoat that smelled of sea salt and cigars. Donato had booked the ten o'clock fitting for a navy ceremonial suit ahead of the Chamber of Commerce gala. [BEAT] Ten minutes later, before Matteo had even chalked the left shoulder, the heavy stairwell door banged open. In walked Carlo Bellini, fifty-nine, lean as a whip, carrying a leather briefcase and a face like thunder. Carlo's eleven o'clock appointment had been moved up by a careless clerk at the port authority, or so he claimed. [LOWER VOICE] The two men had not shared a room since 1986, when a disputed pier lease ended in a shouting match that emptied half a caf\u00e9 on Via Toledo. [PAUSE 1.5s] Donato froze with one arm half-out of his sleeve. Carlo stopped three paces inside the door, his hand still on the brass handle. \"Matteo,\" Carlo said, his voice tight as piano wire, \"tell him to come back Thursday.\" Donato didn't even turn his head. \"Tell him,\" Donato replied, \"that the harbour has only one deep-water berth, and it isn't his.\" [BEAT] Matteo did not look up from the chalk in his hand. He simply gestured toward the second fitting mirror on the far wall. \"Gentlemen,\" Matteo said softly, \"the wool is already cut. If you leave now, the cloth will sit until spring, and neither of you will look good in April.\" Both men stayed, stiff as statues, glaring at each other's reflections.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By eleven-fifteen, the workshop felt airless. Rain had begun to tap against the tall windows, turning the courtyard below into a glistening well of wet stone. Matteo's knees ached from kneeling to mark trouser hems, and his patience was thinning. [LOWER VOICE] Every time he asked Donato to stand straight, Donato shifted his weight to glare at Carlo. Every time he reached for a pin near Carlo's collar, Carlo muttered about port tariffs and crooked lawyers. Matteo's assistant, a twenty-year-old apprentice named Luca, stood near the pressing board looking terrified, clutching a steam iron like a shield. [PAUSE 2s] Matteo stepped back to his cutting table and looked at the two jackets hanging side by side on wooden forms. Something was wrong. Not with the clients\u2014with his own hands. In his haste to keep the peace, he had cut Donato's right shoulder a quarter-inch too wide and Carlo's left lapel a fraction too narrow. [STILLNESS] For thirty years, Matteo had prided himself on never letting noise inside his workshop touch the cloth. Now, the noise had ruined both cuts. If he sent them out like this, the gala would see two badly fitted men, and word would travel through Naples by noon that Matteo Ferraro had lost his eye. [BEAT] He looked at the clock on the wall. Eleven-thirty. Both men were checking their watches, ready to bolt.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] Matteo set the brass shears down with a sharp click that cut through the rain. He did not raise his voice. He simply walked to the center of the room, between the two mirrors, and held up a single length of white basting thread. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"Look at the floor,\" Matteo said. Both men looked down. Between them ran a narrow strip of worn oak floorboards, separating the left mirror from the right. \"You two have spent twelve years arguing over who owns the harbour,\" Matteo said, his voice steady and quiet. [EMPHASIS: a seam does not take sides] \"A seam does not take sides. It only holds two pieces of cloth together so the garment doesn't tear when the wearer moves.\" [BEAT] Carlo scoffed. \"Philosophy from a tailor?\" Matteo didn't blink. He turned to Donato. \"Raise your right arm, Donato. Just to shoulder height.\" Donato hesitated, then lifted it. Matteo turned to Carlo. \"Raise your left.\" Carlo frowned, then complied. Matteo stepped between them, took one end of the white basting thread, and stretched it taut from Donato's cuff to Carlo's. \"Sixty seconds,\" Matteo said. \"Don't speak. Just hold the line.\" [PAUSE 2s] For the first twenty seconds, the only sound was the rain and the hiss of Luca's steam iron. Donato's jaw was clenched. Carlo stared at the wall. At forty seconds, Donato's arm dropped a fraction, and Carlo\u2014without thinking\u2014shifted his wrist slightly to keep the thread from going slack. [WARM SMILE] Matteo watched the thread. It didn't snap. At sixty seconds, Matteo snipped the thread in the middle. \"Now,\" Matteo said, \"we fix the shoulders.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Neither man apologized. Neither man shook hands. [BEAT] Donato stood still while Matteo re-pinned his right shoulder, taking in the quarter-inch he had botched. Carlo stood still while Matteo chalked a cleaner, wider curve on his left lapel. When they left at twelve-fifteen, Donato held the stairwell door open for three seconds longer than he needed to, and Carlo nodded once\u2014barely a centimetre\u2014before stepping into the rain. [PAUSE 1.5s] Three weeks later, at the Chamber of Commerce gala, the harbour contract was signed in a side room before the main course was served. Both men wore navy double-breasted suits. [EMPHASIS: measure the gap, not the grudge] Matteo wasn't at the gala; he was back on the third floor above Piazza San Ferdinando, sweeping wool clippings off the oak floorboards. [STILLNESS] He never told anyone what happened in those sixty seconds, except Luca, who asked him that evening how a piece of thread could quiet two men who hated each other. Matteo didn't look up from his pressing board. [LOWER VOICE] \"It didn't make them friends,\" Matteo said. \"It just gave them something small enough to hold without dropping.\"",
