      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Every April, when the Albany River broke its winter lock, the water did not rise politely. It shoved slabs of grey ice the size of boxcars onto the spruce banks, turned gravel roads into brown canals, and left towns like Kashechewan and Fort Albany sitting like islands in a cold, moving sea. [BEAT] In Timmins, two hundred miles south, Martin Callaghan stood on the wet cedar dock beside his de Havilland Beaver at five-thirty in the morning, smelling aviation fuel, wet pine, and diesel from the generator shed. [PAUSE 1.5s] Martin was fifty-one years old, with knuckles scarred from cowling latches and a habit of folding his flight manifests into crisp, four-inch squares. For nineteen years, he had flown medical supplies, generator parts, and winter mail into the James Bay lowlands. His pride was his thoroughness. Before every departure, Martin liked a forty-minute briefing\u2014weight-and-balance sheets, alternate landing zones, fuel-burn tables, and a full weather-front readout. [EMPHASIS: forty minutes of perfection] was how his dispatcher joked about it. That Tuesday, however, perfection was already underwater. The overnight thaw had breached the dike at Kashechewan. Two clinics were running on backup batteries, three diabetic patients were down to their last vials of insulin, and a squall line was sliding down from Hudson Bay with a ceiling dropping fast toward three hundred feet. Martin climbed into the cockpit, strapped the insulated medical coolers behind the passenger seat, and stared at the fourteen-page regional emergency packet clipped to his yoke. [PAUSE 2s] He told himself that if he just followed the packet line by line, nothing could go wrong. [SCENE SHIFT]\n\n[SPEED UP] An hour and forty minutes north, the world below the floats looked like a shattered mirror. [LEAN IN] Martin banked left over the Albany mouth, squinting through rain streaks on the windshield, and keyed his mic for the Kashechewan nursing station. Static hissed back, sharp as gravel. Then a voice cut through\u2014thin, hoarse, and clipped. It was Nora Desjardins, the thirty-four-year-old regional triage nurse who had been awake since Sunday night. \"Callaghan, if you are reading me, the barge dock is gone,\" Nora said. \"The water took the markers at four a.m. You cannot land by the old chart.\" Martin frowned, thumbing the laminated chart on his knee. \"Nora, give me the current depth at the south channel, the wind gust spread, and confirm the generator fuel reserve,\" he said, falling straight into his standard forty-minute checklist rhythm. [BEAT] On the other end, there was a sharp exhale. \"Martin, the roof is leaking onto the triage table and a kid with a fever is sitting on a folding chair in the hallway,\" Nora snapped. \"I do not have a depth gauge. I have a broomstick and a flashlight.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Martin tried again, reading off the alternate coordinates from page six of the regional packet. \"Check the gravel bar half a mile east\u2014\" \"Underwater!\" Nora cut him off. \"It has been underwater since midnight! Stop reading the binder, Martin. Tell me if you are coming down or turning back, because my radio battery is blinking red.\" [STILLNESS] The Beaver shuddered in a crosswind, and Martin felt his jaw tighten. His whole system\u2014every form, every contingency table\u2014had just been swept away by brown water. [SCENE SHIFT]\n\n[LOWER VOICE] He climbed back to eight hundred feet to buy himself five minutes of clear air above the mist. Rain drummed against the aluminum skin of the cabin like thrownrice. Inside, the cockpit smelled of wet wool and hot oil. Martin looked at the fourteen-page packet on his knee, then at the fuel gauge, then at the dark wall of cloud rolling in from the northwest. [PAUSE 2s] For the first time in nineteen years, his thoroughness felt like dead weight. Every extra minute he spent asking for numbers Nora did not have was a minute the ceiling dropped closer to the treetops. If he turned back to Timmins, the insulin coolers stayed dry, but three people on the ground ran out of time before the next clear window on Thursday. If he dropped blind into the river, he risked flipping the Beaver on a submerged log and becoming a second emergency for a town that had no boat left to reach him. [BEAT] His hands on the yoke were cold. He thought about how easy it was to hide behind a long checklist\u2014to sound competent on the radio while nothing actually moved. [SLOW DOWN] Then he looked down at the dashboard tray and saw a stubby red grease pencil he used for marking fuel drums. [PAUSE 1.5s] He uncapped it with his teeth. [SCENE SHIFT]\n\n[LEAN IN] Martin tore the blank back cover off the fourteen-page packet, pressed it against the yoke, and wrote three words down the left margin: *Spot. Wind. Signal.* [EMPHASIS: three facts, sixty seconds]\u2014that was all the radio battery and the weather window would allow. He keyed the mic, his voice dropping an octave, calm and stripped of every extra syllable. \"Nora, listen,\" Martin said. \"You have sixty seconds before this squall closes the river. Do not read me a chart. Answer three things.\" [BEAT] A click of static. \"Go.\" \"One: where is the widest flat stretch of water without floating timber?\" \"Two hundred yards south of the church steeple, right along the willow line,\" Nora answered instantly. \"Two: which way is the smoke from the clinic chimney blowing?\" \"Straight toward the bay, steady, no swirl.\" \"Three: put someone on the bank with a truck headlight aimed at the water, and flash it twice when the channel is clear.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Silence hung on the frequency for three seconds. Then Nora's voice came back, steadier than before. \"Two hundred yards south of the steeple. Smoke blowing east. Truck is rolling down to the bank right now. Sixty seconds, Martin.\" [WARM SMILE] Martin dropped the flaps, tucked the fourteen-page binder behind his seat where he could not see it, and banked the Beaver into the grey mist. [EMPHASIS: everything else is just noise], he thought, and for the first time all morning, the cockpit felt quiet. [SCENE SHIFT]\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Through the rain, the church steeple appeared like a grey needle, and just south of it, a single pair of truck headlights cut through the gloom, reflecting off a narrow, flat ribbon of brown water. Two sharp flashes. Martin eased the throttle back, let the floats kiss the surface, and rode the spray until the hull settled into a steady, rhythmic slap. [PAUSE 2s] Fifteen minutes later, the insulin coolers were sitting dry on the nursing station counter, and Nora was leaning against the doorframe with a paper cup of lukewarm coffee. Neither of them mentioned the fourteen-page packet. [BEAT] On the flight back to Timmins, with an empty cabin and the squall breaking behind him, Martin did not throw the binder away. He kept it in his flight bag. But taped to the inside of his windshield, right beside the compass, he left the torn back cover with those three words written in red grease pencil. [STILLNESS] [EMPHASIS: he flew the marker, not the manifest]. Whenever a younger pilot asked him how to handle a northern flood, Martin never handed them the manual first. He held up three fingers, tapped the red ink on the glass, and reminded them that in a room\u2014or a cockpit\u2014where everyone is running out of air, the person who wins trust is the one who can say what matters in sixty seconds.",
