      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] On the morning of November fourteenth, the Sawtooth Basin smelled of wet granite and coming snow. [BEAT] Callum MacLeod stood beside the tailgate of a county pickup at the Iron Creek trailhead, his gloved thumb resting on the rim of a dented steel thermos. At fifty-eight, Callum had spent thirty-two winters reading what elk, wolverines, and lost tourists left behind in the duff. His knees clicked when the barometer dropped, and his canvas coat carried the permanent resin stain of lodgepole pine. [PAUSE 1.5s] Across the gravel lot, two halogen floodlights hissed against a freezing drizzle, illuminating a folding map table where Deputy Sheriff Dana Kowalski was pinning laminated topo sheets against the wind. Somewhere above nine thousand feet, a thirty-four-year-old structural engineer from Boise named Nadia Okafor had failed to check in at dusk. Her rental sedan sat thirty yards away, frost spidering across its windshield, a half-eaten granola bar on the passenger seat. [EMPHASIS: The clock was already twelve hours deep into hypothermia weather.]\n\n[SPEED UP] Within twenty minutes, the staging area had split into two camps. Deputy Kowalski had three county searchers, a thermal-imaging drone team from Twin Falls, and a pair of bloodhounds whining in the back of a crate. Callum had a braided leather lanyard, a brass pocket magnifier, and a pair of reading glasses tucked into his flannel pocket. [BEAT] \"We're running the thermal bird up the main granite chute at first light,\" Kowalski said, tapping a red dry-erase circle on the map. \"If she took the high switchback to beat the cloud deck, she's tucked under a boulder shelf.\" Callum leaned over the table, his eyes tracing the contour lines where the timberline thinned into scree. \"She didn't take the high switchback,\" he said quietly. \"Look at the mud by the trail register. A size-eight trail runner, shallow tread, heel strike angling west. She turned off before the first footbridge.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] The drone pilot, a twenty-six-year-old named Trevor, zipped his flight case shut with a sharp metallic rasp. \"With respect, Mr. MacLeod, a heel print in the mud doesn't tell us where she is four miles up. Our thermal rig covers four hundred acres an hour. You're talking about crawling through brush.\" Kowalski glanced between them, her jaw tight. \"We'll run the chute,\" she decided. \"Callum, you want to walk the creek bank, take a radio. Stay on channel three.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By eleven that morning, the drizzle had thickened into sleet that rattled like dry rice against Callum's hood. The thermal drone had grounded twice with iced rotors, and the bloodhounds had lost the scent at the first snowmelt crossing. Callum was three miles up the west fork, his calves burning, his notebook soaked through at the corners. [LOWER VOICE] For two hours, he had found nothing\u2014no dropped glove, no candy wrapper, no boot print in the frozen mud. Every elk trail looked like a human detour; every wind-felled branch looked like a broken crutch. [BEAT] Doubt crept in behind his ribs, cold and familiar. Maybe Trevor was right. Maybe a man with a magnifying glass was just an artifact from another century, stubbornly staring at dirt while the mountain swallowed someone whole. Callum stopped beneath a cluster of subalpine firs to catch his breath, his radio spitting static from his collar. Over channel three, Kowalski's voice sounded thin and frayed: \"Base to all units, ceiling's dropping to eight thousand. We're pulling the dogs back to the trucks.\" [PAUSE 2s] Callum stared at the gray wall of sleet closing the valley. If they pulled the search until dawn, Nadia Okafor would not see morning. [STILLNESS] He exhaled slowly, watching his breath vanish, and forced himself to stop looking for a person. Instead, he looked at the ankles of the forest.\n\n[LEAN IN] [EMPHASIS: Three millimeters of pale wood.] That was what caught his eye\u2014not a footprint, not a torn jacket, just a single dry twig on a low-hanging fir bough, snapped halfway through at shin height. The bark on the underside was still intact, acting like a tiny hinge, and the exposed inner wood was bright cream, not yet darkened by the sleet. Callum dropped to one knee in the wet needles, ignoring the sting in his joint. [BEAT] \"You didn't break from snow load,\" he murmured to the twig. Snow bends a branch downward; this snap angled upward and to the left, twisted by something brushing past in a hurry. He keyed his mic. \"Dana, this is Callum. Hold the dogs at the bridge. I've got fresh sign at the west fork.\" Static answered him, then Kowalski's clipped voice: \"Callum, a broken stick in a windstorm isn't sign. We're losing light.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] \"It's not wind,\" Callum said, his voice dropping into the calm, flat register he used when the trail spoke clearly. \"Wind snaps brittle wood clean. This is a green-hinge tear, eighteen inches off the ground, pointing upslope toward the old logging cut. [EMPHASIS: Direction is written in the snap.] Give me thirty minutes.\" On the other end, silence stretched for four long heartbeats. Then: \"Thirty minutes, Callum. Not a second more.\"\n\n[SPEED UP] From that first twig, the forest began to talk. Fifty yards upslope, Callum found a cluster of dried huckleberry stems bent at a uniform forty-degree angle, their tips pointing away from the creek. Twenty yards past that, a single lichen-covered stone had been rolled half an inch in its bed, exposing a crescent of black soil. [BEAT] He wasn't tracking a hiker anymore; he was reading a rhythm\u2014the uneven stride of someone tired, favoring her left ankle, pushing through brush with her right forearm. Every forty feet, there was a tiny broken twig, a bruised fern frond, a scrape on a fallen log. [WARM SMILE] Then, just below a granite overhang where the wind howled through a chimney of rock, the twigs stopped. Callum froze, scanning the gray timber. [PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] Ten feet to his left, wedged into the hollow under a cedar root, a bright blue sleeve moved\u2014just a fraction of an inch. Nadia Okafor was conscious, shivering violently, her right ankle swollen inside a laced boot. When Callum knelt beside her and wrapped his dry wool sweater around her shoulders, she looked up through sleet-matted hair and whispered, \"How did you find me? I didn't even have a whistle.\" Callum glanced back down the slope, where the wind was already erasing the faintest marks in the duff. [EMPHASIS: \"You left a trail,\" he told her softly. \"Just not the kind anyone else was looking for.\"] Two hours later, as the litter team carried her into the halogen glow of the trailhead, Trevor stood beside his grounded drone case, watching Callum pour the last of his cold coffee into the gravel. Nobody said a word about four hundred acres an hour.",
