      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] At 2:14 a.m. on a Tuesday in November, the fourth-floor waiting room at St. Jude\u2019s Mercy in Cleveland smelled of burnt decaf and wet wool. [PAUSE 1.5s] Rain ticked against the tall, double-paned glass overlooking Euclid Avenue, where sodium streetlights turned the puddles the color of old brass. [BEAT] Miriam Okafor, fifty-four years old, eighteen years a night-shift hospital chaplain, stood by the nurses\u2019 station with a clipboard tucked under her arm and a paper cup of lukewarm tea in her hand. Her shoes were orthopedic, scuffed at the toes from miles of linoleum. Every night followed a rhythm she knew by heart: check the census, walk the quiet corridors, sit with the families who could not sleep, and keep her voice low enough that the monitors remained the loudest thing in the room. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tonight, however, the rhythm broke at Bed 4 in ICU-B. Frank Kowalski, eighty-six, a retired tool-and-die maker, had suffered a massive hemorrhagic stroke at dinner. His chart listed two next of kin: Arthur Kowalski, sixty-one, and David Kowalski, fifty-seven. [EMPHASIS: Eleven years of practiced silence] separated them. They had not spoken a word to each other since the afternoon their mother\u2019s probate closed in 2015. Now, both men were sitting in the same twenty-by-twenty waiting room, separated by four empty vinyl chairs, staring at opposite walls while Dr. Patel prepared to come out and ask them for a joint decision on comfort care. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SPEED UP] Miriam walked into the waiting room and took the single blue fabric chair near the vending machine, equidistant from both brothers. Arthur wore a faded Carhartt jacket, his knuckles scarred from forty years of lathe work. David wore a pressed charcoal raincoat, a leather laptop bag resting between his loafers. [BEAT] Miriam introduced herself softly, offered water, and watched Arthur\u2019s jaw tighten. \"He\u2019s my father too,\" Arthur said, not looking at David. \"I\u2019m the one who drove him to every chemo appointment in \u201909.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] David\u2019s eyes stayed fixed on the muted television bolted to the ceiling. \"And I\u2019m the one who paid the property taxes on the house for six years so the city wouldn\u2019t take it,\" David replied, his voice clipped, rehearsed. [LOWER VOICE] Miriam tried the standard chaplain\u2019s bridge\u2014reminding them that Frank was resting, that the medical team needed them aligned, that tonight was about their father\u2019s dignity. [BEAT] Arthur\u2019s fist hit the armrest once, a dull thud. \"Don\u2019t lecture me about dignity, Reverend. He knows what he did.\" David stood up, grabbed his bag, and walked to the far window. \"Tell the doctor to call my cell when he\u2019s ready,\" David said. \"I\u2019m not sitting here taking inventory of the past.\" [EMPHASIS: Nobody wins a waiting-room war], Miriam thought, watching the glass reflect two men who looked almost identical in profile\u2014the same sloped shoulders, the same stubborn set of the chin\u2014refusing to share the same oxygen. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SLOW DOWN] [LOWER VOICE] At 3:05 a.m., Miriam stepped back into the corridor to check with the charge nurse. Dr. Patel was scrubbing out of a procedure down the hall and would be in the waiting room in less than five minutes. [BEAT] Miriam leaned against the cool cinderblock wall, feeling the familiar weight behind her eyes. Eighteen years of training told her to mediate, to validate both sides, to give each brother space to be heard. Yet every minute she gave them to explain their grievances only hardened the concrete between them. If Dr. Patel walked in while they were still trading eleven-year-old receipts, the decision would fracture, and one of them would carry that regret for the rest of his life. [PAUSE 1.5s] She looked down at her clipboard. Tucked behind the intake sheet was a creased intake note from the ambulance crew: *Patient found in garage. Clutching a green thermos.* [STILLNESS] Miriam\u2019s breath caught. She remembered Frank Kowalski\u2019s admission note from two years ago, when he\u2019d been in for a hip replacement\u2014a note she had jotted in the margin about two boys and a Saturday morning fishing trip. She had [EMPHASIS: sixty seconds before the doors opened]. No time for a mediation session. No time for a ten-minute sermon on forgiveness. Only enough time for one sharp, undeniable truth. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] Miriam walked back into the waiting room and did not sit down. She stood in the center of the tile floor, right between the blue chair and the window. \"Gentlemen,\" she said, her voice steady, \"Dr. Patel will be through those doors in one minute. I\u2019m not going to ask you to forgive each other tonight.\" [BEAT] Both men looked up, surprised by the lack of a plea. [SLOW DOWN] \"Two years ago,\" Miriam said, \"your father told me about a Saturday morning in October 1974, when he took both of you out to Pier 14 before sunrise. He said the lake was choppy, and he only had one [EMPHASIS: grease-stained thermos] of hot chocolate. He said Artie dropped his rod into the water, and David\u2014who was nine\u2014jumped halfway over the railing to grab it by the reel before it sank. Your father said he grabbed David by the belt, pulled him back, and split that single cup of hot chocolate three ways in the dark.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Miriam\u2019s eyes moved from David\u2019s face to Arthur\u2019s. \"He didn\u2019t tell me about the house. He didn\u2019t tell me about the taxes. He told me that when he\u2019s scared, he still remembers the sound of two boys laughing in the dark on Pier 14.\" [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s] For ten seconds, only the hum of the vending machine filled the room. Arthur\u2019s shoulders dropped an inch. He looked at the floor, then at David\u2019s shoes. \"It was a Zebco 33,\" Arthur muttered, his voice cracking. \"You nearly went in after it.\" David\u2019s hand tightened on the strap of his laptop bag, then let go. \"You would\u2019ve killed me if I\u2019d lost it,\" David said quietly. [WARM SMILE] He walked back from the window\u2014not all the way, just enough to take the chair two seats down from Arthur, leaving [EMPHASIS: just enough room for two] coats between them. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[LOWER VOICE] When the double doors swung open at 3:12 a.m. and Dr. Patel stepped in with his tablet, he did not find two men at opposite ends of a room. He found two brothers sitting side by side, leaning forward, ready to listen. [BEAT] They agreed on comfort care in four minutes. Before sunrise, Arthur and David stood on either side of Bed 4, each holding one of Frank Kowalski\u2019s hands as the monitor\u2019s green line flattened into a quiet hum. [PAUSE 2s] Miriam did not stay for the paperwork. She slipped out into the hallway, set her empty paper cup in the bin, and walked toward the elevators. She had learned something that night that no seminary class had ever taught her: when people are drowning in a decade of their own noise, you don\u2019t throw them a textbook. You hand them a sixty-second memory\u2014clean, specific, and true\u2014and step out of the way.",
