      "craftBreakdown": {
        "setupSummary": "Nora Halvorsen arrives at her flooded Duluth basement radio studio at 5:15 a.m. with four guests who are determined to deliver long, jargon-heavy monologues while rising water threatens to trip the main breaker.",
        "turnOrMisdirection": "Instead of trying to fix the sump pump or rush the guests through their prepared notes, Nora kills all four microphones mid-broadcast and forces each panelist to deliver one concrete, 60-second personal story from that morning.",
        "payoffOrTag": "The breaker trips three seconds after she signs off, proving that four sharp 60-second stories outlasted a doomed 30-minute panel\u2014and permanently changed how she ran the show.",
        "whyItLands": "It makes the case for brevity physical and urgent: the rising water is a ticking clock that mirrors every crowded conference panel where speakers waste time on setup instead of human truth."
      },
      "tags": [
        "third-person",
        "panel-speaking",
        "brevity-under-pressure"
      ]
