      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On the third Tuesday of April in Duluth, Minnesota, the St. Louis River did not crest the way the county hydrologist had promised it would. [BEAT] By three-fifteen in the morning, the storm drain outside the basement window of WDLH-FM had backed up, and [EMPHASIS: three inches of muddy river water] was creeping across the linoleum toward the rack of aging broadcast amplifiers. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nNadia Kowalski, the station's senior morning producer, stood on an overturned milk crate with her wool socks tucked into rubber galoshes. At thirty-eight, Nadia had spent twelve years turning messy, meandering civic debates into crisp morning radio. Her desk was a monument to control: three color-coded grease pencils, a mechanical stopwatch she trusted more than the digital wall clock, and a laminated card taped above the mixing board that read, \"If it takes longer than a traffic light, cut it.\" [WARM SMILE] That morning, however, the traffic light was underwater, and her usual three-person morning crew was stranded on the other side of a washed-out bridge.\n\n[LEAN IN] The county emergency manager had just called the studio line. A levee two miles upriver was seeping, and four neighborhoods needed to know which evacuation routes were still open before the five-o'clock commuters turned their keys in the ignition. Nadia had the map, she had the sheriff's dispatch notes, and she had a backup diesel generator humming in the alley\u2014until a floating pallet slammed against the basement stairwell and shorted the main breaker. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SPEED UP] The studio lights died. Only the amber glow of the emergency battery bank remained, casting long, trembling shadows across the soundproofing foam. Nadia's headset crackled. Through the static came the voice of her engineer, Marcus Delgado, calling from the transmitter shack three miles up the ridge. \"Nadia, the basement UPS is down to eleven percent,\" Marcus said, his voice clipped. \"You've got maybe four minutes of clean power before the board drops. Do you want me to switch to the automated weather loop?\"\n\nNadia looked at the grease-stained notepad in her hand. She had [EMPHASIS: forty-two minutes of tape] and bullet points\u2014historic flood levels from 1972, quotes from the mayor's press secretary, a breakdown of sandbag distribution sites, and the actual street-by-street detour map. If she handed the airwaves over to the automated loop, listeners would hear a robotic voice reading yesterday's barometer pressure while water rose over their driveways. [BEAT] \"No,\" Nadia told Marcus. \"Give me the mic. I'll run the whole morning brief.\"\n\n\"Nadia, you can't run a forty-minute brief on four minutes of battery,\" Marcus shot back. \"By the time you finish thanking the county clerk, the transmitter's going to eat dead air. Give me the three street names and let me read them.\"\n\n[LOWER VOICE] \"People don't trust a voice they don't recognize at four in the morning,\" Nadia said, stepping off the milk crate into the cold water. It sloshed over the tops of her galoshes. \"They know my voice. Just keep the stick hot.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nShe pulled the heavy dynamic microphone toward her face and tapped the spacebar on the studio laptop. Nothing happened. The laptop's screen had gone black\u2014water had wicked up the power brick. Her digital script, with all its neatly timed transitions and phonetic pronunciations, was gone. All she had left was the soggy yellow legal pad and a wall clock whose red second hand was sweeping past 4:12. [BEAT]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By 4:14, the smell of wet ozone filled the room. Nadia's hands were shaking, not from the cold, but from the sudden, suffocating realization that she had spent her entire career editing other people's words and had never once had to deliver an unscripted emergency broadcast while standing in a rising river. She tried to organize the legal pad in her head. First, establish credibility. Second, explain why the levee was seeping. Third, thank the volunteers at the firehall. Fourth, list the detours. [SLOW DOWN] She started talking into the dead mic just to rehearse the opening, and thirty seconds vanished before she even reached the first street name. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n\"Nadia!\" Marcus's voice snapped through her right ear. \"Battery just dropped to four percent. The inverter's whining. You don't have four minutes. You have [EMPHASIS: sixty seconds of clean air], max. Then the relay trips and we're off the air until the utility crew gets here. Cut the history lesson.\"\n\n[STILLNESS] Nadia froze. Sixty seconds. On a normal morning, she gave sixty seconds just to the farm report. Her thumb hovered over the red \"ON AIR\" button. Every instinct she had\u2014every journalistic habit of context, attribution, and thoroughness\u2014screamed that sixty seconds wasn't enough to be responsible. If she left out the mayor's statement, she'd get a call by noon. If she skipped the sandbag hours, people would show up at the wrong gate. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nThen she looked up at the laminated card above the board, half-peeling in the damp air. \"If it takes longer than a traffic light, cut it.\" [LEAN IN] She realized, with a jolt that steadied her breathing, that [EMPHASIS: nobody remembers the setup when the roof leaks]. Listeners in their kitchens didn't need to know why the levee was seeping or who was to blame. They needed to know where to drive and where not to drown. Everything else was ego disguised as thoroughness.\n\nNadia grabbed her thickest black grease pencil. She didn't cross out words; she drew a single diagonal slash through the top three-quarters of the yellow page. She left only three lines: two closed bridges, one open high-ground route, and the address of the high school gym. [BEAT] She clicked the mechanical stopwatch in her left hand, pressed the red button with her right, and watched the tally light flare.\n\n[LOWER VOICE] [SLOW DOWN] \"Good morning, Duluth. This is Nadia Kowalski at WDLH. It is four-fifteen, and our basement is flooded, so you have me for one minute.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Her voice was steady, almost conversational, stripped of every radio-host flourish. \"Do not take the Oliver Bridge or Highway 23\u2014both are underwater as of ten minutes ago. If you are leaving the lower valley, take Skyline Parkway east to the high school gym. Doors are open, coffee is on, and the Red Cross is already there. Again: avoid Oliver Bridge, avoid Highway 23, take Skyline Parkway east. Stay dry, and check on your neighbor before you pull out of the driveway.\"\n\nShe glanced at the stopwatch. Fifty-four seconds. She didn't add a sign-off. She didn't pad the remaining six seconds with a weather tease. She simply clicked the mic off, and [EMPHASIS: the red light stayed on] for three more quiet heartbeats before the inverter gave a final, hollow sigh and the studio went completely dark. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[WARM SMILE] [SCENE SHIFT] Three hours later, when the sun came up over a brown, swirling lake where the parking lot used to be, Nadia climbed the flooded stairs to the street. Her phone had forty-two text messages. None of them asked about the mayor's press secretary. None of them asked about the 1972 flood crest. Half of them were from listeners who had heard that single, sixty-second cut on their car radios and turned around before reaching the Oliver Bridge. The other half were from fellow producers across the state, asking how she had managed to sound so calm with no script and no lights. Nadia stood on the wet curb, clicked her mechanical stopwatch once to zero it out, and finally took off her waterlogged galoshes.",
